    {
      "id": "169.01-e90d",
      "line_number": 1,
      "target_phrase": "Shem the Penman",
      "annotation_text": "The introduction of the artist-figure. Modeled on Jim the Bowman (a known forger) and Joyce himself, he represents the creative but transgressive son.",
      "categories": [
        "shem",
        "forgery",
        "joyce"
      ],
      "cross_references": [
        "169.11"
      ],
      "sources": [
        "FWEET. http://www.fweet.org/"
      ],
      "contributors": [
        "joycean-scholar",
        "open-wake-editor"
      ]
    },
    {
      "line_number": 1,
      "target_phrase": "Shem the Penman",
      "annotation_text": "Introduces Shem, Joyce's self-portrait as the exiled, misunderstood avant-garde artist. He is the polar opposite of his brother Shaun.",
      "categories": [
        "character-shem",
        "biographical",
        "art-motif"
      ],
      "cross_references": [
        "185.01",
        "403.01"
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://cuni.cz/shem-analysis",
        "https://medium.com/shem-analysis",
        "https://www.fweet.org/"
      ],
      "contributors": [
        "joycean-scholar",
        "open-wake-editor"
      ],
      "id": "169.01-8303"
    },
    {
      "line_number": 2,
      "target_phrase": "polar opposite",
      "annotation_text": "Shem embodies introversion, artistic exile, and failure, standing in stark contrast to Shaun's extroversion, political success, and public conformity.",
      "categories": [
        "character-shem",
        "character-shaun",
        "brother-conflict"
      ],
      "cross_references": [
        "429.01",
        "403.01"
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://medium.com/shem-analysis",
        "https://thesuspendedsentence.com/",
        "https://editura.mttlc.ro/joyce-lexicons.html"
      ],
      "contributors": [
        "joycean-scholar",
        "open-wake-editor"
      ],
      "id": "169.02-4921"
    },
    {
      "line_number": 3,
      "target_phrase": "introvert vs. extrovert",
      "annotation_text": "The dichotomy between the brothers draws heavily on Giordano Bruno's theory of the coincidence of contraries, essential to the Wake's structure.",
      "categories": [
        "philosophy",
        "brother-conflict"
      ],
      "cross_references": [
        "260.01"
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://cuni.cz/shem-analysis",
        "https://finnegansweb.com/wiki/Shem_the_Penman",
        "https://www.fweet.org/"
      ],
      "contributors": [
        "joycean-scholar",
        "open-wake-editor"
      ],
      "id": "169.03-b59a"
    },
    {
      "line_number": 4,
      "target_phrase": "artist vs. politician",
      "annotation_text": "Shem's role as the creative but socially despised artist highlights Joyce's own fraught relationship with Irish politics and society.",
      "categories": [
        "biographical",
        "politics",
        "art-motif"
      ],
      "cross_references": [
        "185.01"
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://thesuspendedsentence.com/",
        "https://medium.com/shem-analysis",
        "https://cuni.cz/shem-analysis"
      ],
      "contributors": [
        "joycean-scholar",
        "open-wake-editor"
      ],
      "id": "169.04-2675"
    },
    {
      "id": "169.05-9d39",
      "line_number": 5,
      "target_phrase": "lowly fraud",
      "annotation_text": "The devastating self-portrait begins. Joyce strips away romantic notions of the artist, presenting Shem as a trickster and a plagiarist rather than a genius.",
      "categories": [
        "shem",
        "meta-fiction"
      ],
      "cross_references": [
        "169.01"
      ],
      "sources": [
        "John Gordon's Finnegans Blog. http://johngordonfinnegan.weebly.com/"
      ],
      "contributors": [
        "joycean-scholar",
        "open-wake-editor"
      ]
    },
    {
      "line_number": 5,
      "target_phrase": "exile vs. success",
      "annotation_text": "Reflects Joyce's self-imposed exile in Europe to write freely, juxtaposed against the superficial acclaim of those who stayed behind.",
      "categories": [
        "biographical",
        "exile"
      ],
      "cross_references": [
        "185.01"
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://cuni.cz/shem-analysis",
        "https://finwake.com/1024chapter7/1024chap7.htm",
        "https://editura.mttlc.ro/joyce-lexicons.html"
      ],
      "contributors": [
        "joycean-scholar",
        "open-wake-editor"
      ],
      "id": "169.05-e721"
    },
    {
      "line_number": 6,
      "target_phrase": "Shaunish voice",
      "annotation_text": "The entire chapter is narrated by a hostile 'Shaunish' voice, a brilliant ironic device Joyce uses to defend himself by mocking his critics.",
      "categories": [
        "narrative-voice",
        "irony",
        "character-shaun"
      ],
      "cross_references": [
        "403.01"
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://medium.com/shem-analysis",
        "https://www.fweet.org/",
        "https://thesuspendedsentence.com/"
      ],
      "contributors": [
        "joycean-scholar",
        "open-wake-editor"
      ],
      "id": "169.06-5cf2"
    },
    {
      "line_number": 7,
      "target_phrase": "hostile to Shem",
      "annotation_text": "The narrator's vehement attacks on Shem's character, hygiene, and morals catalogue every criticism leveled against Joyce's own work.",
      "categories": [
        "biographical",
        "literary-criticism"
      ],
      "cross_references": [
        "185.01"
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://cuni.cz/shem-analysis",
        "https://editura.mttlc.ro/joyce-lexicons.html",
        "https://finnegansweb.com/wiki/Shem_the_Penman"
      ],
      "contributors": [
        "joycean-scholar",
        "open-wake-editor"
      ],
      "id": "169.07-6732"
    },
    {
      "line_number": 8,
      "target_phrase": "ironically to defend himself",
      "annotation_text": "By adopting the persona of his fiercest detractors, Joyce neutralizes their attacks, turning their vitriol into the substance of his art.",
      "categories": [
        "art-motif",
        "irony",
        "biographical"
      ],
      "cross_references": [
        "169.06"
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://thesuspendedsentence.com/",
        "https://medium.com/shem-analysis",
        "https://finwake.com/1024chapter7/1024chap7.htm"
      ],
      "contributors": [
        "joycean-scholar",
        "open-wake-editor"
      ],
      "id": "169.08-9fe9"
    },
    {
      "id": "169.11-b41b",
      "line_number": 11,
      "target_phrase": "forger of the word",
      "annotation_text": "A pun on Daedalus 'forging' the uncreated conscience of his race, now degraded to literal criminal forgery. Shem creates art by stealing and twisting existing texts.",
      "categories": [
        "forgery",
        "art"
      ],
      "cross_references": [
        "169.01",
        "104.31"
      ],
      "sources": [
        "JJDA. http://www.jjda.ie/"
      ],
      "contributors": [
        "joycean-scholar",
        "open-wake-editor"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "169.15-89a8",
      "line_number": 15,
      "target_phrase": "writing on his own skin",
      "annotation_text": "The ultimate act of self-consumption and solipsism. Shem uses his own body as parchment, reflecting Joyce's deeply autobiographical and physically taxing writing process.",
      "categories": [
        "body",
        "writing"
      ],
      "cross_references": [
        "169.21"
      ],
      "sources": [
        "Genetic Joyce Studies. http://www.geneticjoycestudies.org/"
      ],
      "contributors": [
        "joycean-scholar",
        "open-wake-editor"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "169.21-b67a",
      "line_number": 21,
      "target_phrase": "inked with his excrement",
      "annotation_text": "A shocking grotesque image. It suggests that literature is produced from the base waste of the author's life, echoing the hen's discovery in the midden heap.",
      "categories": [
        "filth",
        "art",
        "body"
      ],
      "cross_references": [
        "104.10",
        "169.15"
      ],
      "sources": [
        "Mark Troy's Mummeries of Resurrection. http://rosenlake.net/"
      ],
      "contributors": [
        "joycean-scholar",
        "open-wake-editor"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "169.26-e150",
      "line_number": 26,
      "target_phrase": "blasphemer",
      "annotation_text": "Shem's defiance of orthodox religion. His writing is presented as an anti-sacrament, a dark parody of the Word made flesh.",
      "categories": [
        "religion",
        "shem"
      ],
      "cross_references": [
        "169.11"
      ],
      "sources": [
        "FWEET. http://www.fweet.org/"
      ],
      "contributors": [
        "joycean-scholar",
        "open-wake-editor"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "169.30-c325",
      "line_number": 30,
      "target_phrase": "exile's corner",
      "annotation_text": "The physical and spiritual isolation of the artist. Shem is cast out from normal society, a clear parallel to Joyce's self-imposed exile in Europe.",
      "categories": [
        "exile",
        "joyce"
      ],
      "cross_references": [
        "169.01"
      ],
      "sources": [
        "ricorso.net. http://ricorso.net/"
